# Warnings

Strike talks back. When a directive activates but asks for something the
renderer cannot do literally, the page still renders — and a line goes to
stderr saying what happened instead.

This page is deliberately full of mistakes. Serve the site and watch the
terminal:

```sh
strike serve docs
```

Nothing on this page is broken. Every group below still forms, every section
still renders, and every citation mark still appears. A warning is information,
not an error — which is the point: a layout mistake in the middle of a long
document must never cost you the document.

> [!NOTE]
> Warnings are per *document*, printed when the page is rendered. Under
> `--watch` you get a fresh set each time a file changes, which makes the
> terminal a decent live linter while you write.

## A grid with the wrong number of sections

`grid(2)` with three sections renders all three — the third wraps onto a second
row — and warns.

// mismatch grid(2)

**One**

// --

**Two**

// --

**Three, which wraps**

// end mismatch

```
group 'mismatch': grid(2) but 3 section(s)
```

## A single command that has nothing to arrange

`/grid(2)` applies to one content element, and one element has no sections to
put in columns.

/grid(2)

A lone paragraph under a `/grid(2)` line.

```
single command: grid(2) but 1 element
```

## The layout-level rule

The same layout command twice along one chain of containers: the inner one is
dropped, the group still forms, and the warning names the command.

// outer_grid grid(2)

// inner_grid grid(2)

**The inner grid is stripped**

This group is real — it just has no `grid` on it, so its content stacks
instead of splitting.

// --

**Its sections still exist**

Structure never depends on nesting depth. That is what makes the rule safe.

// end inner_grid

// --

**The outer grid still works**

Only the colliding command is dropped, and only on the inner group.

// end outer_grid

```
group 'inner_grid': grid ignored (already inside a grid)
```

The same rule reaches a `/cmd()` chain, which is why `/skinny() /skinny()`
warns once about the inner one.

## Citations

The [Citations](citations.sx) page has a working bibliography. Here are the
ways one can go wrong. Every mark below renders as ordinary text with an inert
reference.

A [claim citing entry 99].cite(99) of three, a
[claim citing an unknown key].cite(nosuchkey), and a
[claim citing both at once].cite(99, nosuchkey).

First, a `citations()` group with no numbered list in it. There is nothing for
the command to declare, so the group renders as an ordinary group:

// empty_citations citations()

No numbered list here, so no entries. This does *not* use up the document's one
citations slot — a later group can still claim it, which the next one does.

// end empty_citations

Then the real bibliography, written with three problems at once: it starts at 3
rather than 1, and two entries claim the same key.

// bad_citations citations()

3. First entry — the numbers below are what the author wrote.
4. [dup] Second entry.
5. [dup] Third entry, claiming a key the second already took.

// end bad_citations

Entries always bind by **position**, 1..n, whatever numbers the list carries.
That is why a list starting anywhere but 1 is worth a warning: the numbers a
reader sees would otherwise disagree with the numbers marks bind to.

Finally, a second `citations()` group. The document already has one, so this
keeps its content and loses the command:

// second_citations citations()

1. This list is never a bibliography.

// end second_citations

All together, in the order they are reported:

```
group 'empty_citations': citations ignored (no numbered list in the group)
citations: the entry list starts at 3; entries still bind as 1..3
citations: duplicate key [dup] (the first entry wins)
group 'second_citations': citations ignored (the document already has a citations group)
cite(99): only 3 entries
cite(nosuchkey): unknown key
cite(99): only 3 entries
cite(nosuchkey): unknown key
```

The last four come in pairs because the third mark cites both bad references at
once — marks are reported per reference, not per mark.

## The one you will not see here

`2 citation mark(s) but no citations group` needs a document with marks and no
bibliography anywhere, which this page is not. It is reported once for the whole
document rather than once per mark.
